# PayPal Checkout System - Cursor Rules

## Project Context
This is a production-ready PayPal checkout system with Next.js 14 frontend and Express backend.

## Code Standards

### Security First
- Never hardcode credentials
- Always validate input on server side
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Implement proper error handling
- Don't leak sensitive information in errors

### Code Style
- Use clear, descriptive variable names
- Add comments for complex logic
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Use async/await over promises

### Backend (Express)
- All API endpoints should validate input
- Use proper HTTP status codes
- Log important events for audit trail
- Return consistent JSON response format
- Handle PayPal SDK errors gracefully

### Frontend (Next.js)
- Use 'use client' directive for client components
- Keep sensitive operations on backend
- Validate user input before API calls
- Show loading states during async operations
- Provide clear error messages to users

## File Organization

### Backend Structure
```
backend/
├── src/
│   ├── config/      # Configuration (PayPal, etc.)
│   ├── controllers/ # Business logic
│   ├── routes/      # API route definitions
│   ├── middleware/  # Custom middleware
│   └── utils/       # Helper functions
└── server.js        # Entry point
```

### Frontend Structure
```
frontend/
├── app/             # Next.js App Router pages
├── components/      # Reusable React components
└── lib/             # Utilities and helpers
```

## Common Tasks

### Adding a New API Endpoint
1. Create controller function in `backend/src/controllers/`
2. Add route in `backend/src/routes/`
3. Add input validation
4. Add error handling
5. Test with Postman or similar

### Adding a New Page
1. Create page in `frontend/app/[page-name]/page.js`
2. Add 'use client' if using hooks
3. Use existing styling patterns
4. Add loading states
5. Add error handling

### Modifying PayPal Integration
- Always test in sandbox mode first
- Check PayPal documentation for changes
- Update both frontend and backend if needed
- Test complete flow after changes

## Testing Checklist

Before committing:
- [ ] Test in sandbox mode
- [ ] Check for console errors
- [ ] Verify error handling
- [ ] Test with different amounts
- [ ] Test payment flow end-to-end
- [ ] Check responsive design
- [ ] Verify environment variables work

## Deployment Checklist

Before deploying:
- [ ] Update environment variables
- [ ] Switch to production PayPal credentials
- [ ] Enable HTTPS
- [ ] Configure CORS for production
- [ ] Set up webhooks with production URL
- [ ] Test complete flow in production
- [ ] Monitor logs for errors

## Security Guidelines

- Never commit `.env` or `.env.local` files
- Keep Client Secret on backend only
- Validate all user input
- Use HTTPS in production
- Implement rate limiting
- Verify webhook signatures
- Log security events
- Rotate credentials regularly

## Documentation

- Update README.md for major changes
- Add comments for complex logic
- Document API endpoint changes
- Update environment variable documentation
- Keep security documentation current

